module

public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Sites.Over
public import Sheaf.CategoryTheory.Sites.GrothendieckTopos
import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Sites.Coverage

/-!
# Localization of a topology generated by chosen-pullback stable presieves

Source: Mathlib, `CategoryTheory/Sites/Over.lean`, lines 540--571, with the coverage
induction of `CategoryTheory/Sites/Coverage.lean`, lines 197--203 and 262--264
(blueprint: lem-per-generated-topology-slice).

The chosen pullbacks are quantified directly, so no closure under arbitrary isomorphic
pullback objects, indexed presentations or composition is imposed.
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

namespace CategoryTheory

/-- Localization commutes with generating a Grothendieck topology from a precoverage
stable under one chosen pullback of each arrow of a designated presieve.

The index `R.uncurry` contains each arrow of `R` with its domain. The maps `p` and `q`
are respectively the first and second projections of the chosen pullbacks; only the
presieve of these second projections is required to belong to `K`.

Source: Mathlib, `CategoryTheory/Sites/Over.lean`, lines 540--571, replacing the stronger
base-change class by the chosen-pullback hypothesis; Stacks Project, Sites, Tags 00Y0,
00Z1 and 00Z4 (blueprint: lem-per-generated-topology-slice).

Proof plan:
1. Identify presieves and sieves over `Y ⟶ T` with those on `Y`. The structure map on
   every domain is forced. This identification preserves top, pullbacks and generated sieves.
2. The chosen second projections form a designated presieve factoring through `R` along
   `f`, by the commutativity of each pullback square. Thus `K` satisfies the coverage condition
   of `lem-per-coverage-generated-sieve-induction`.
3. The localized topology contains the slice generators, so minimality gives the inclusion
   from the topology generated on the slice into the localized topology
   (`def-weil-pbc-topos`).
4. For the reverse inclusion use `Coverage.Saturate.rec`, the recorded declaration of
   `lem-per-coverage-generated-sieve-induction`, with the motive that for every map to `T`
   the corresponding slice sieve covers. Generator and top cases follow from step 1.
5. In the transitive case evaluate the induction hypothesis at the composite structure
   map on each domain. Step 1 identifies the resulting pullback sieve, and local character
   (`def-weil-pbc-topos`) proves it covers. No composition or isomorphic-replacement
   stability is used.
-/
theorem over_toGrothendieck_eq_comap_of_chosen_pullbacks
    {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] (K : Precoverage C)
    (hK : ∀ (Y Y' : C) (R : Presieve Y), R ∈ K Y → ∀ (f : Y' ⟶ Y),
      ∃ (P : R.uncurry → C) (p : ∀ i, P i ⟶ i.val.1) (q : ∀ i, P i ⟶ Y'),
        (∀ i, IsPullback (p i) (q i) i.val.2 f) ∧ Presieve.ofArrows P q ∈ K Y')
    (T : C) :
    K.toGrothendieck.over T = (K.comap (Over.forget T)).toGrothendieck := by
  sorry

end CategoryTheory
